      "title": "Session cookies deliberately allowed over plain HTTP",
      "technical_explanation": "The configuration disables the Secure cookie flag so login sessions work on plain-HTTP LAN or reverse-proxy deployments without TLS. The code comments show this is an intentional, documented trade-off rather than a mistake. On a plain-HTTP network, a network-level observer could read the session cookie, but this is a deployment-transport risk the operator chooses by running without TLS, and the project's threat model is self-hosted local use. No attacker-controlled path is demonstrated by the supplied evidence.",
      "layman_explanation": "The app lets you stay logged in even when running on a network without encryption. That's a deliberate convenience choice; if someone runs it over an untrusted network without HTTPS, a snooper on that network could theoretically see the login cookie. For typical home or localhost use this is not a practical problem.",
      "developer_action": "Consider gating the Secure-cookie setting on whether the configured base URL uses HTTPS, or documenting that users exposing the app over a network should put TLS in front of it.",
